Question 20: Traffic Is Not the Whole Story

AIM: Plot vehicles vs PM2.5, color by zone, scale by population.

Code:
import pandas as pd
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt

stations = pd.DataFrame({
    'station':['S1','S2','S3','S4','S5','S6','S7','S8'],
    'vehicles_k':[35,40,65,60,45,50,70,30],
    'pm25':[58,60,80,77,110,115,83,52],
    'zone':['Residential','Residential','Commercial','Commercial',
            'Industrial','Industrial','Commercial','Residential'],
    'population_k':[40,45,30,25,15,10,35,50]})

colors = {'Residential':'blue','Commercial':'green','Industrial':'red'}
stations['color'] = stations['zone'].map(colors)

plt.scatter(stations['vehicles_k'], stations['pm25'],
            c=stations['color'], s=stations['population_k']*5, label=None)

for zone,color in colors.items():
    plt.scatter([],[],c=color,label=zone)

plt.xlabel("Vehicles (k)")
plt.ylabel("PM2.5")
plt.title("Traffic vs Pollution")
plt.legend()
plt.show()


Output:
Scatter plot with colored zones and scaled sizes.

Explanation:  
Functions used: scatter(), map(), legend(), xlabel(), ylabel(), title()
Usage: scatter() to plot points, map() to assign colors, legend() to show zones, xlabel() and ylabel() to label axes, title() to add chart title
Interpretation: Industrial stations S5 and S6 record PM2.5 above 100 despite only 45–50k vehicles, while the busiest station S7 (70k vehicles) records only 83.
Traffic is not the sole driver; industrial activity explains the anomaly.
